ise, I must pass.\n\nNow, strategic choice: use high straight to beat, or pass to let teammate keep pressure?\n\nBut if I pass, teammate gets to lead next — they can start a new trick. That could be good — they may have a plan.\n\nBut if the landlord has the rocket or a bomb, they could interrupt.\n\nAlternatively, if I play the straight, I remove high cards and possibly help deplete my hand.\n\nBut I risk wasting a high combo when not necessary.\n\nMore importantly: my teammate played a straight ending at K — they likely don’t have an Ace-high straight themselves. If they did, they might have played it already.\n\nBy passing, I signal that I cannot beat their play — which is true only if I don’t have the straight, but I do.\n\nBut in practice, peasants should avoid overtaking each other unless necessary.\n\nStandard strategy: let your teammate run if they’re leading and the landlord is passing.\n\nHere, teammate played, landlord passed — they’re in control.\n\nIf I now beat their play, I’m effectively stealing the lead — which is poor cooperation.\n\nEven though I beat a landlord pass, the last active play was by teammate — I’m responding to their lead? No.\n\nClarify: the "Last Play to Beat" is ♥9 ♥10 ♥J ♣Q ♣K — played by teammate (next player), right?\n\nWait — look:\n\n"Turn 3 (Next Player): ♥9 ♥10 ♥J ♣Q ♣K"\n\nYes — so the teammate played this.\n\nThen "Turn 3 (Prev Player): Pass" — landlord passed.\n\nNow it\'s my turn — I must either beat that play or pass.\n\nBut the play I must beat is from my **teammate**, not the landlord.\n\nAh! Critical point!\n\nThe teammate led a trick (9-K straight), landlord couldn\'t beat it, passed.\n\nNow I, as partner, must either beat my teammate’s play or pass.\n\nBut why would I want to beat my own teammate?\n\nIn Dou Dizhu, you never beat your teammate’s successful play unless you’re forced (e.g., misreading).\n\nBut here, the teammate played a winning move — landlord passed — so the trick is effectively won by teammate.\n\n